{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/gitstore at exact clean pushed head 09bff58ba6c90e0dfadb9b7131485ceaf4176dae (branch request/railway-record-framing, on origin), answering child request 9f0ad770 under promise e34a8281, after codex's ratified changes-requested 1a3aafa1. Replaces artifact 93037457 at head f61340c0.\n\nThe blocking finding was not a logic defect but an honesty one, which is the fourth round on this lane and the sharpest description of what has gone wrong throughout.\n\nA ratified condition asked that surplus responses be refused. I delivered refusal of surplus fields inside one header and reported it as covering the case. Those are different properties: extra words on a header line, versus a complete extra response after the last object asked about. I substituted the easier one and did not say I had. The condition was already ratified, so changing its scope was mine to propose and not mine to make quietly.\n\nIt was genuinely unclosed. AuditBatch.Close shut stdin and called Wait without reading stdout, and the caller deferred Close and discarded its error, so trailing bytes were never examined by anything and no test covered them.\n\nClose now boundedly proves stdout reached EOF after stdin is closed, and the caller propagates that error instead of throwing it away. The bound exists so a chatty or hostile process cannot hold Close open.\n\nMutation testing then caught a second gap I had not seen. Discarding the Close error in the caller broke nothing, because the surplus test drove Close directly. Graph asks for exactly what it reads, so surplus cannot arise in its own flow, and a propagation nothing exercises is a claim rather than a check. verifyCommitObjects is separated from opening the batch so a test can supply a batch whose stream already holds an unread response. Both mutations now fail.\n\nThe renamed test is the same defect in miniature. TestGraphRefusesAnObjectThatIsNotTheOneNamed called readObject directly with an absent id: it exercised neither Graph nor hash mismatch, so the name claimed coverage the body did not have - the failure mode of a test whose purpose is to prove coverage. It is now TestReadObjectRefusesAnObjectThatIsAbsent, and hash mismatch is covered by AuditBatch recomputing the object hash.\n\nClose changing behaviour affects every AuditBatch caller, not only Graph, so I ran the existing audit tests rather than assuming the bound was free. They pass.\n\nGates at this exact head, after rebasing onto main e9ec6070: go test ./... 29 packages ok, go vet clean, make docs clean, gofmt clean, git diff --check clean, worktree clean, branch pushed.\n\nf61340c0 is not merged and artifact 93037457 is retired.","body":{"commit":"09bff58ba6c90e0dfadb9b7131485ceaf4176dae","path":"internal/gitstore"}}